update posts set
  title = 'Why Luxury Real Estate Content Needs a Completely Different Strategy',
  excerpt = 'Luxury property isn''t sold on square footage — it''s sold on identity. Most content strategies for high-end real estate still don''t understand the difference.',
  category = 'Strategy',
  published_at = '2026-01-12T09:00:00Z',
  body_json = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most real estate content makes the same mistake: it leads with features. Four bedrooms. Three bathrooms. Twenty-four hundred square feet. Private pool. Sea view. That is spec-sheet marketing, and it does not sell luxury property."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Luxury Is an Emotion, Not a Feature List"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Someone looking at a multi-million dollar property is rarely making a spreadsheet decision. They are making an identity decision. The content has to answer one question before anything else: is this consistent with who I am, or who I want to be seen as? That means the photography, the copy, the pacing of a video, even the music underneath it, all have to communicate status and taste before they communicate square footage."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Channel Mix Changes Completely"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A fifteen-second Reel that works beautifully for a clinic or a restaurant falls flat for a multi-million dollar villa. The luxury buyer is not scrolling for entertainment in quite the same way — they are on LinkedIn, in private WhatsApp groups, watching long, cinematic 4K walkthroughs on YouTube at their own pace. Meeting them where they already are matters more than chasing whatever platform is trending that quarter."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The formats that tend to actually convert at this end of the market are fairly consistent across the industry:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cinematic 60–90 second property films, not slideshow-style listings"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Neighbourhood and lifestyle content that sells the life around the property, not just the property"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Developer or builder credibility content — who built this, and what else have they built"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investment and ROI framing for buyers thinking about the asset, not just the address"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"None of this is complicated in theory. It is just slower and more deliberate than most content calendars are built for, which is exactly why so few brands do it well. The ones that do treat every piece of content as a small proof point for a much bigger feeling, and let the specs live in the brochure, not the headline."}]}]}'::jsonb
where slug = 'why-luxury-real-estate-content-needs-a-different-strategy';

update posts set
  title = 'My AI Workflow for Managing Multiple Brands',
  excerpt = 'How a handful of AI tools changed the day-to-day of running marketing across several brands at once — without the output quality dropping.',
  category = 'AI & Productivity',
  published_at = '2026-01-27T09:00:00Z',
  body_json = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Running marketing for more than one brand at a time used to mean choosing between speed and quality. You could move fast and let consistency slip, or slow everything down to protect it. That trade-off is a lot less brutal now, mostly because AI tools have quietly become part of the actual production process rather than just a novelty."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What Actually Changed"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The honest answer is that AI did not replace strategy. It replaced the slow, repetitive parts around strategy — the first draft of a caption, the fifth variation of a headline, the rough visual concept before a designer touches it, the auto-captions on a reel edit. None of that felt like a bottleneck on its own, but it adds up fast across multiple brands, and it is exactly the kind of work AI tools are now genuinely good at."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Tools That Earned a Permanent Spot"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Claude","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — strategy documents, longer-form copy, campaign briefs, keeping a brand''s voice consistent across a lot of output"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ChatGPT","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — quick caption variations, hashtag research, fast ideation when you just need options"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Midjourney","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — concept visuals and moodboards before a designer starts real production"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Canva''s AI features","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — template production at scale and resizing the same creative across platforms without redoing it manually"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"CapCut''s AI tools","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — reel editing, auto-captions, template-based video that used to eat an afternoon"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The pattern that works is roughly the same every week: plan and brief early, produce in batches rather than one piece at a time, review everything against brand guidelines before it goes anywhere, and only then schedule it out. AI compresses the timeline in the middle of that process. It does not skip the planning at the start or the review at the end — the moment you let it, quality drops fast."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The result is not that everything happens faster for the sake of speed. It is that more of the week is available for the parts that actually need a person — judgement calls, brand instinct, deciding what not to publish — instead of being eaten by production admin."}]}]}'::jsonb
where slug = 'my-ai-workflow-for-managing-multiple-brands';
